1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,445
Every Java beginner has the same first week.

2
00:00:03,445 --> 00:00:04,048
Red text.

3
00:00:04,048 --> 00:00:05,052
The word error.

4
00:00:05,052 --> 00:00:06,056
A little arrow.

5
00:00:06,056 --> 00:00:07,796
And no idea what it means.

6
00:00:08,346 --> 00:00:10,493
Three messages cause most of it.

7
00:00:10,493 --> 00:00:11,768
Semicolon expected.

8
00:00:11,768 --> 00:00:12,841
Class is public,

9
00:00:12,841 --> 00:00:15,793
should be declared in a file with that name.

10
00:00:15,793 --> 00:00:18,074
Could not find or load main class.

11
00:00:18,624 --> 00:00:19,579
Today you write,

12
00:00:19,579 --> 00:00:21,488
compile and run a small program,

13
00:00:21,488 --> 00:00:22,920
you break it on purpose,

14
00:00:22,920 --> 00:00:25,664
and you learn to read each message in seconds.

15
00:00:26,214 --> 00:00:29,265
After this, red text is not scary. It is a map.

16
00:00:31,315 --> 00:00:35,518
Four small rules explain almost every beginner failure. Learn them once.

17
00:00:36,068 --> 00:00:36,325
One.

18
00:00:36,325 --> 00:00:39,350
The public class name must match the file name,

19
00:00:39,350 --> 00:00:40,959
capital letters included.

20
00:00:40,959 --> 00:00:43,598
Greeter dot java holds the class Greeter.

21
00:00:44,148 --> 00:00:44,416
Two.

22
00:00:44,416 --> 00:00:47,160
A program starts in a method called main.

23
00:00:47,160 --> 00:00:47,628
Public,

24
00:00:47,628 --> 00:00:48,097
static,

25
00:00:48,097 --> 00:00:48,431
void,

26
00:00:48,431 --> 00:00:50,506
and a string array called args.

27
00:00:51,056 --> 00:00:51,499
Three.

28
00:00:51,499 --> 00:00:53,344
java C wants a file name,

29
00:00:53,344 --> 00:00:54,377
with dot java.

30
00:00:54,377 --> 00:00:56,148
Java wants a class name,

31
00:00:56,148 --> 00:00:57,477
with no dot class.

32
00:00:58,027 --> 00:01:03,062
Four. Every word you type after the class name arrives in args, one word at a time.

33
00:01:03,612 --> 00:01:08,326
Keep these four in mind. Now let us watch them work, on a real Ubuntu machine.

34
00:01:10,376 --> 00:01:13,363
Go to the shop folder. Type: CD, shop.

35
00:01:13,913 --> 00:01:17,390
Here is our program. Type: cat, Greeter dot java.

36
00:01:17,940 --> 00:01:19,400
A class named Greeter,

37
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:19,997
and main.

38
00:01:19,997 --> 00:01:21,325
If no name is given,

39
00:01:21,325 --> 00:01:24,643
it prints a usage message and exits with code two.

40
00:01:24,643 --> 00:01:27,497
Otherwise it greets the first word in args.

41
00:01:28,047 --> 00:01:28,487
First,

42
00:01:28,487 --> 00:01:29,514
the quick way.

43
00:01:29,514 --> 00:01:34,575
Java twenty-one can compile and run one source file in a single step.

44
00:01:34,575 --> 00:01:35,382
Type: java,

45
00:01:35,382 --> 00:01:36,629
Greeter dot java,

46
00:01:36,629 --> 00:01:36,922
Ada.

47
00:01:37,472 --> 00:01:41,845
Hello, Ada. Handy for quick tests. Nothing was saved, so let us check.

48
00:01:42,395 --> 00:01:43,611
Type: L S.

49
00:01:44,161 --> 00:01:48,428
Only the source files. No class file. The compile happened in memory.

50
00:01:48,978 --> 00:01:54,162
Now the real way, step one: compile. Type: java C, Greeter dot java.

51
00:01:54,712 --> 00:01:57,379
Silence means success. Type: L S.

52
00:01:57,929 --> 00:02:00,702
Greeter dot class is here. That is the bytecode.

53
00:02:01,252 --> 00:02:06,905
Step two: run it. Class name only, then a word for args. Type: java, Greeter, Ada.

54
00:02:07,455 --> 00:02:12,447
The same greeting, now from the saved bytecode. Ada was args, position zero.

55
00:02:12,997 --> 00:02:15,856
What if we give no name? Type: java, Greeter.

56
00:02:16,406 --> 00:02:17,625
The program refuses,

57
00:02:17,625 --> 00:02:19,149
and prints how to use it.

58
00:02:19,149 --> 00:02:21,343
That message went to standard error,

59
00:02:21,343 --> 00:02:22,379
not standard out.

60
00:02:22,929 --> 00:02:27,495
Now the number every pipeline reads. Type: echo, dollar, question mark.

61
00:02:28,045 --> 00:02:28,314
Two.

62
00:02:28,314 --> 00:02:30,599
The exit code of the last command.

63
00:02:30,599 --> 00:02:31,877
Zero means success.

64
00:02:31,877 --> 00:02:33,759
Anything else means failure.

65
00:02:33,759 --> 00:02:36,045
This is how a build knows to stop.

66
00:02:38,095 --> 00:02:41,615
Now we break things. Go to the shop. Type: CD, shop.

67
00:02:42,165 --> 00:02:46,432
This file has a typo. Type: java C, Broken dot java.

68
00:02:46,982 --> 00:02:48,444
Read it in four parts.

69
00:02:48,444 --> 00:02:50,638
First: file name and line number.

70
00:02:50,638 --> 00:02:51,967
Then the word error,

71
00:02:51,967 --> 00:02:53,031
and the message.

72
00:02:53,031 --> 00:02:54,294
Then the code line.

73
00:02:54,294 --> 00:02:57,883
And the arrow: it points to where java C got confused.

74
00:02:58,433 --> 00:03:02,294
One error. And no class file was made, because the compile failed.

75
00:03:03,344 --> 00:03:06,692
The statement on line three needs a semicolon.

76
00:03:06,692 --> 00:03:08,075
We add it with sed.

77
00:03:08,075 --> 00:03:08,803
Type: sed,

78
00:03:08,803 --> 00:03:09,313
dash I,

79
00:03:09,313 --> 00:03:09,749
quote,

80
00:03:09,749 --> 00:03:10,332
three S,

81
00:03:10,332 --> 00:03:10,768
slash,

82
00:03:10,768 --> 00:03:11,278
dollar,

83
00:03:11,278 --> 00:03:11,715
slash,

84
00:03:11,715 --> 00:03:12,442
semicolon,

85
00:03:12,442 --> 00:03:12,879
slash,

86
00:03:12,879 --> 00:03:13,316
quote,

87
00:03:13,316 --> 00:03:14,480
Broken dot java.

88
00:03:15,030 --> 00:03:18,785
Compile again. Type: java C, Broken dot java.

89
00:03:19,335 --> 00:03:22,236
Silence. Now run it. Type: java, Broken.

90
00:03:22,786 --> 00:03:26,925
It works. One missing character, and the whole program refused to build.

91
00:03:27,475 --> 00:03:28,694
A second mistake.

92
00:03:28,694 --> 00:03:30,558
This file is called Wrong,

93
00:03:30,558 --> 00:03:33,211
but the class inside is called Right.

94
00:03:33,211 --> 00:03:34,143
Type: java C,

95
00:03:34,143 --> 00:03:35,219
Wrong dot java.

96
00:03:35,769 --> 00:03:37,132
Class Right is public,

97
00:03:37,132 --> 00:03:40,478
and should be declared in a file named Right dot java.

98
00:03:40,478 --> 00:03:42,894
That is rule one: the names must match.

99
00:03:43,444 --> 00:03:47,306
And a run-time mistake. Type: java, Greeter dot class.

100
00:03:47,856 --> 00:03:50,134
Could not find or load main class.

101
00:03:50,134 --> 00:03:51,676
We typed the file name.

102
00:03:51,676 --> 00:03:54,223
Rule three: java wants the class name,

103
00:03:54,223 --> 00:03:55,429
with no dot class.

104
00:03:57,479 --> 00:04:01,767
Let us sort what you saw. Two kinds of failure, and they happen at different moments.

105
00:04:02,317 --> 00:04:03,442
At compile time,

106
00:04:03,442 --> 00:04:05,831
java C complains about the source.

107
00:04:05,831 --> 00:04:06,815
Syntax errors,

108
00:04:06,815 --> 00:04:08,432
and the file name rule.

109
00:04:08,432 --> 00:04:10,189
No class file is created.

110
00:04:10,739 --> 00:04:11,540
At run time,

111
00:04:11,540 --> 00:04:14,744
java complains about what you asked it to start.

112
00:04:14,744 --> 00:04:15,879
Wrong class name,

113
00:04:15,879 --> 00:04:16,746
wrong folder,

114
00:04:16,746 --> 00:04:19,550
or the program itself exiting with a code.

115
00:04:20,100 --> 00:04:22,548
Your own program can fail on purpose,

116
00:04:22,548 --> 00:04:23,408
like Greeter.

117
00:04:23,408 --> 00:04:25,261
It writes to standard error,

118
00:04:25,261 --> 00:04:27,246
and sets a non-zero exit code.

119
00:04:27,796 --> 00:04:32,020
Always ask first: did it compile, or did it run? Then read the message.

